  "message": "I checked with toyota_mechanic: **DIAGNOSIS (nothing changed):**\n\nIn the sandbox copy of Toyota's code, the `browser_opener` wiring is broken at a more basic level than \"how it works\" — it can't even be imported right now. The sandbox copies `receptionist_team`, `finance_team`, `agent_memory`, and `tests`, but not the sibling folder `lancedb_memory/sssf_interfaces`, which `receptionist_team.agents` needs (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'sssf_interfaces'`). That one missing import breaks 7 of 11 test modules — basically everything that touches `roles.py`, `extra_roles.py`, `main.py`, `service.py`, and the routing model, which is the whole `browser_opener` wiring path.\n\nMechanic's take on the fix: copying the single file `sssf_interfaces/__init__.py` into the sandbox (it's pure stdlib, locates its real target via absolute paths already used elsewhere) should unblock those 7 modules with no logic changes.\n\nSeparately, there's currently **no dedicated test file** for `browser_opener` itself (`test_browser_opener_role.py` doesn't exist), so even once imports are fixed, its route→folder→model.json wiring has zero direct test coverage.\n\nNothing was changed. If you'd like, I can run the mechanic again (without `--diagnose`) to apply that sandbox-copy fix and add the missing test.",
